Only the component of the angular momentum along the axis of rotation is considered, so
$
  L = vb(L) dot vu(n) = omega sum_i m_i (vu(n) times vb(x_i)) dot (vu(n) times vb(x_i)) = omega I.
$
If the torque is also along the axis of rotation, $vb(G) = G vu(n)$, then dotting $vb(dot(L)) = vb(G)$ with $vu(n)$ gives
$
  G = I dot(omega).
$
Hence $G$ acts like a _rotational force_, causing change in the angular velocity.
